A||||2||Pretender||bolt patterns and some questions about the brakes||||6||
Z||000000||Pretender||08-02-2000||06:33 AM||||i'm going to try and fiddle around with my braking system (88GT). I need to find front rotors that are larger then stock and still have the same bolt pattern. What is our bolt pattern? what is the factory size of my rotors? how large are the grand am rotors that all the guys who have 84-87's upgrade to? and finally what is the difference between the 88's and the others? vented rotors? is that all? thanks, in advance, shawn.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Raydar||08-02-2000||08:55 AM||||Shawn, Our bolt pattern is 5x100 mm. <BR>AFAIK, the Grand Am disks are about the same size as the 88 Fiero. You might check Ryane Motorsports, PISA, or V-8 Archie for upgrades. Other members might have some better suggestions. <BR>88 brakes are usually pretty good. If yours doesn't stop decently, there's probably something wrong. (BTW... CarboMet pads SUCK until they get hot.)<BR>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."||||reg||1
Z||000002||lawrence||08-02-2000||12:56 PM||||The diameter of the 88 rotors is 10.4".  The 88-89 LeBaron has the same bolt pattern, but the diameter is only a bit larger, I think 11" (maybe less).  The problem with this rotor is that it has the e-brake drum built into it.<P>When I did my 88 brake upgrade, I didn't want to use this rotor because a) it's heavy as hell, and b) the offset is the same as the Fiero rotor, so there would be no physical space for the caliper mounting brackets I had to make.<P>The advantage I (we) have with the 88s is that the calipers are very small.  I was able to mount 11.5" rotors from a 97 Firebird using the stock calipers, and they fit under the stock 15" wheels.  I actually could have put a 12" rotor in the rear, if I had found one.<P>So far, this is the best mechanical improvement I have made to this car.  Before, I was almost afraid to drive it.  Now, I charge the corners hard, then brake at the last possible moment.  I have that much confidence.<P>I will agree with the comment that the CarboMets aren't good until they warm up.  In fact, I'm thinking of going back to OEM pads.  I just hate to throw these away since they are practically brand new.  I will say that they make NO noise and the brake dust created is very low.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
